- You missed Halloween, Dane!
- I know, Elise, I'm sorry,


00:28:52.998 --> 00:28:55.838
I couldn't help it.
I had to make a call.


00:28:55.878 --> 00:28:58.078
You should have seen
the look on the kid's faces.


00:28:58.118 --> 00:29:00.320
Guilt doesn't work on me, Elise.
I had to make a call.


00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:02.960
A call. A call! You always
have to make a stinking call.


00:29:02.999 --> 00:29:06.159
If I was an O.B. would I have
to listen to "a baby, a baby,


00:29:06.199 --> 00:29:08.799
you always have to deliver a
stinking baby"? It's what I do.


00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:11.321
You think I wanted to miss tonight?


00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:12.920
That I like making those calls?


00:29:12.961 --> 00:29:15.640
I know you like making those calls!


00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:18.120
Sometimes I feel like you
don't really live here,


00:29:18.161 --> 00:29:19.520
you just sleep here.


00:29:19.561 --> 00:29:22.601
- Like a favorite song...
- It's sad.


00:29:22.641 --> 00:29:25.842
It's just Halloween, Elise.


00:29:25.881 --> 00:29:28.001
How sad can a bunch of dressed-up kids


00:29:28.042 --> 00:29:29.801
with a bellyful of candy actually be?


00:29:29.843 --> 00:29:34.363
Sad for you, Dane.
You're missing it.


00:29:34.402 --> 00:29:36.522
Even when you're here
you're not really here.


00:29:36.563 --> 00:29:39.923
You're always thinking about
some deal you've got going,


00:29:39.964 --> 00:29:41.843
or some candidate you're scamming.


00:29:41.884 --> 00:29:46.523
One day, you're gonna wish
you had this time back.


00:29:46.564 --> 00:29:50.644
I love you and it makes me sad.
For you.


00:29:50.685 --> 00:29:53.724
I'm tired, Elise.


00:29:53.764 --> 00:29:56.524
What do you say we list
my flaws in the morning?


00:30:10.847 --> 00:30:13.766
You know, I work damn hard
for this family.


00:30:13.806 --> 00:30:16.846
I bust my ass seventy hours
a week in that boiler room


00:30:16.887 --> 00:30:19.326
to make enough money
so that you can stay at home.


00:30:19.366 --> 00:30:20.848
And I appreciate...


00:30:20.887 --> 00:30:22.526
You think it's easy
working commission sales?


00:30:22.527 --> 00:30:25.567
Cold calling?
Starting with a blank slate


00:30:25.607 --> 00:30:28.287
the first of every month?
Waking up at 2 AM


00:30:28.327 --> 00:30:31.928
with a knot in my gut hoping
to God I can close a deal?


00:30:31.969 --> 00:30:35.328
And all so my family
can have a nice life.


00:30:35.368 --> 00:30:38.009
And if that means
missing a fringe holiday


00:30:38.048 --> 00:30:40.409
and being the bad guy,
then that's a sacrifice


00:30:40.449 --> 00:30:42.369
- I'm happy to make!
- Dane...


00:30:42.409 --> 00:30:47.129
- Dane...
- I'm a goddamned American hero.


00:30:51.050 --> 00:30:54.090
You did not just call yourself
an American hero.


00:30:57.530 --> 00:31:00.531
I oversold it, didn't I?


00:31:04.371 --> 00:31:07.331
I can't even stay mad at you!


00:31:07.371 --> 00:31:10.332
You're such an asshole sometimes!


00:31:10.373 --> 00:31:14.493
Ow! Sometimes? Whoa!


00:31:14.532 --> 00:31:18.092
Get over here, American hero!


00:31:24.733 --> 00:31:27.973
Mrs. Jensen, your
pediatrician was correct


00:31:28.013 --> 00:31:30.374
to act so expeditiously.


00:31:30.414 --> 00:31:33.574
Ryan's blood tests show
a platelet level alarmingly low.


00:31:33.614 --> 00:31:36.454
We need to conduct
blood tests to determine...


00:31:36.494 --> 00:31:37.814
Wait a minute, wait a minute,
wait a minute...


00:31:37.815 --> 00:31:39.935
Don't you think we might just be


00:31:39.975 --> 00:31:42.535
jumping the gun a bit?
My wife brings him in


00:31:42.575 --> 00:31:45.095
because he's probably got
some bug that he picked up


00:31:45.135 --> 00:31:47.575
from any one of a hundred kids
and we go straight from that...


00:31:47.615 --> 00:31:50.136
To this?


00:31:50.176 --> 00:31:52.176
Let's just step back a second, alright?


00:31:52.217 --> 00:31:53.776
I wish I had the luxury


00:31:53.816 --> 00:31:55.696
of time to step back, to be giving you


00:31:55.736 --> 00:31:58.377
the warm and furries. I don't.


00:31:58.416 --> 00:32:02.577
And, more importantly,
I suspect your son doesn't.


00:32:02.617 --> 00:32:05.057
He's probably just...
He's getting fat


00:32:05.098 --> 00:32:06.377
because he sits on his ass


00:32:06.417 --> 00:32:08.377
playing Assassin's Creed all day.


00:32:08.417 --> 00:32:11.018
He's not fat, Mr. Jensen.


00:32:11.058 --> 00:32:13.098
His spleen is swollen.


00:32:13.138 --> 00:32:16.378
What?


00:32:16.418 --> 00:32:18.578
My initial examination of Ryan


00:32:18.618 --> 00:32:22.380
revealed petechiae and purpura,
which you thought


00:32:22.419 --> 00:32:24.699
were bruises.


00:32:24.739 --> 00:32:27.219
He complains of chronic fatigue and...


00:32:27.259 --> 00:32:31.340
- You did not notice those signs?
- "Signs"?


00:32:31.380 --> 00:32:33.740
Of what...?


00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:37.180
I believe your son has A.L.L.


00:32:37.220 --> 00:32:39.541
Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia.


00:32:45.621 --> 00:32:48.621
"Believe?"
As in "guessing?"


00:32:48.661 --> 00:32:50.582
As in "I don't really know
what he's got?"


00:32:50.622 --> 00:32:53.862
"Believe" as in "based on 233 children


00:32:53.902 --> 00:32:56.022
I've treated with A.L.L."


00:32:56.062 --> 00:32:59.063
You see, the good news is
that it's the most common


00:32:59.103 --> 00:33:00.543
form of childhood cancer


00:33:00.583 --> 00:33:02.903
and we have a great deal
of experience treating it.


00:33:02.943 --> 00:33:05.263
The realistic news is that it gets worse


00:33:05.303 --> 00:33:07.743
very quickly if left untreated.


00:33:07.784 --> 00:33:11.464
You... believe it's this A.L.L.


00:33:11.504 --> 00:33:15.424
but you're not sure, how could you be?


00:33:15.464 --> 00:33:19.145
Right? He could be fine?


00:33:19.185 --> 00:33:21.705
Whatever his condition, Mrs. Jensen,


00:33:21.745 --> 00:33:23.945
he is not fine.


00:33:30.066 --> 00:33:33.466
Alright, Ryan.


00:33:33.506 --> 00:33:36.867
You can hop out now, buddy.
Sorry, hospital policy.


00:33:36.906 --> 00:33:39.507
Sure somebody's not using this room?


00:33:39.547 --> 00:33:42.587
Oh, my bad. Those are Billy's,
he's not with us anymore.


00:33:43.907 --> 00:33:47.347
He went home Monday!
He got better in no time,


00:33:47.387 --> 00:33:49.428
just like you will, right?


00:33:49.468 --> 00:33:53.908
Hey! Look, Rhino, you got your own TV.


00:33:53.948 --> 00:33:56.508
- Better than being at home, huh?
- Yeah, his own TV,


00:33:56.548 --> 00:34:00.149
private bathroom, and oh!


00:34:00.189 --> 00:34:02.869
If you push this, you not
only get to control the TV,


00:34:02.909 --> 00:34:04.149
but you get your very own


00:34:04.189 --> 00:34:06.749
room-service button too, huh.
- What?


00:34:06.789 --> 00:34:10.230
I think I'm gonna have to catch
a little leukemia myself.


00:34:12.430 --> 00:34:15.630
Well, I don't know about that,
Dad, but I'm an aide,


00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:19.151
so a nurse will be here any sec, you guys
need anything, you just holler at me.


00:34:19.191 --> 00:34:20.111
My name's Antoine.


00:34:20.151 --> 00:34:21.551
- Okay.
- Alright?


00:34:21.591 --> 00:34:24.031
- See you later, bud.
- Thanks.


00:34:40.393 --> 00:34:42.793
- Hey, Dad?
- Yeah?


00:34:42.833 --> 00:34:45.113
When's Mom coming back?


00:34:45.153 --> 00:34:47.793
She'll be back as soon as she
finishes the insurance paperwork


00:34:47.833 --> 00:34:50.634
downstairs.


00:35:22.317 --> 00:35:24.957
With the insurance that we have now,


00:35:24.997 --> 00:35:27.437
the cost of my medication alone,


00:35:27.477 --> 00:35:32.438
is as much as a car payment.
Your dad, he's...


00:35:32.478 --> 00:35:35.398
He's trying so hard, but...


00:35:35.438 --> 00:35:37.678
I'm very worried.


00:35:37.718 --> 00:35:41.279
Thank you, sweetheart,
and thank Greg for me,


00:35:41.319 --> 00:35:44.838
and please make sure you
let him know it's just a loan.


00:35:44.879 --> 00:35:48.920
Okay, kiss the grandkids for me.


00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:52.960
- Daddy?
- Yeah?


00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:55.120
When will Mommy and Ryan be home?


00:35:55.160 --> 00:35:57.961
Oh...


00:35:58.001 --> 00:36:01.801
Mommy and Ryan are
staying at the hospital,


00:36:01.841 --> 00:36:04.241
but they'll be home soon
when Ryan feels better.


00:36:04.281 --> 00:36:06.761
Hm?


00:36:06.801 --> 00:36:09.801
Grandma Inez is gonna come
stay with us, though.


00:36:09.842 --> 00:36:14.242
You and Nathan will
like that, won't you? Okay.


00:36:14.282 --> 00:36:16.402
Alright.


00:36:16.442 --> 00:36:19.203
Goodnight, sweetie.


00:36:22.683 --> 00:36:26.122
- Daddy?
- Yeah?


00:36:26.163 --> 00:36:29.324
I forgot to brush my teeth.


00:36:29.364 --> 00:36:31.804
Let me see 'em.


00:36:33.404 --> 00:36:36.844
They're baby teeth.
They're all gonna fall out anyway.


00:36:36.884 --> 00:36:40.005
Go to sleep, pumpkin.


00:37:06.367 --> 00:37:09.288
- Dane Jensen.
- Sorry I'm calling so late.


00:37:09.327 --> 00:37:11.208
Hey, Lou.


00:37:11.247 --> 00:37:14.488
It's just that I haven't heard
from you or this Firestone fella


00:37:14.528 --> 00:37:17.529
in a while.
You know how it is.


00:37:17.568 --> 00:37:20.609
Wife's gettin' tired of me
hangin' around the house all the time.


00:37:20.649 --> 00:37:24.369
- What can I do for you, Lou?
- Well,


00:37:24.409 --> 00:37:27.490
if you come across any entry
level engineer openings,


00:37:27.530 --> 00:37:29.730
I'd like to hear about it.


00:37:29.769 --> 00:37:31.850
You know, maybe we can
both make some money.


00:37:31.890 --> 00:37:34.330
Companies don't pay us to recruit


00:37:34.370 --> 00:37:36.010
for entry level engineers.


00:37:37.851 --> 00:37:40.211
I'm sorry, man. I've been having
some personal problems.


00:37:40.251 --> 00:37:41.969
What do you say we pick
this up in the morning?


00:37:41.970 --> 00:37:43.531
Everything alright?


00:37:43.571 --> 00:37:46.090
Yeah, yeah... family issues.
I'll figure it out.


00:37:46.131 --> 00:37:49.651
- Wanna talk about it?
- Do I want to talk about it?


00:37:49.692 --> 00:37:54.252
Dane, I've got three kids,
two daughters and a son.


00:37:54.292 --> 00:37:56.772
They're all grown-up now,
but they were a handful.


00:37:56.812 --> 00:37:59.173
Two grandkids. I've been married


00:37:59.213 --> 00:38:01.452
29 years, and it hasn't always
been a walk in the park.


00:38:01.493 --> 00:38:05.733
- Maybe I can help.
- Ah, it's okay, Lou.


00:38:05.773 --> 00:38:08.814
I'm handling it.
I'll corral Sumner,


00:38:08.853 --> 00:38:11.694
and he'll be back in the hunt
first thing in the AM, okay?


00:38:11.733 --> 00:38:14.893
You take your time,
do what you gotta do.


00:38:14.934 --> 00:38:18.895
Don't worry about me.
It's all good here. But, Dane?


00:38:18.935 --> 00:38:21.935
- Yeah?
- Remember something...


00:38:21.975 --> 00:38:26.175
Every family has its issues.
But you only have one family.


00:38:26.215 --> 00:38:29.776
Like it, Lou. Like it.


00:38:29.816 --> 00:38:32.936
Take care.


00:38:32.976 --> 00:38:35.376
You too.


00:38:54.218 --> 00:38:57.539
- Ryan, look who's here!
- Hey, Dad.


00:38:57.579 --> 00:39:00.899
Rhino, how you doing, buddy?


00:39:00.939 --> 00:39:04.499
Fine.


00:39:04.538 --> 00:39:07.060
He's almost asleep.
This is when you show up?


00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:10.660
- Dr. Singh was in earlier.
- And?


00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:13.580
And I need you here to help ask


00:39:13.619 --> 00:39:15.180
the questions I don't always know which
questions to ask him.


00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:17.516
- Alright, alright, calm down.


00:39:17.555 --> 00:39:18.915
I'm gonna go get something to eat.


00:39:18.940 --> 00:39:20.620
You'll be here a while?


00:39:20.661 --> 00:39:22.701
Yeah. Where else would I be, Elise?


00:39:22.741 --> 00:39:25.581
I'll be right back, baby.


00:39:25.621 --> 00:39:27.302
Okay, Mom.


00:39:37.983 --> 00:39:40.183
She gone?


00:39:40.222 --> 00:39:43.743
Yeah. Yeah, she's gone.


00:39:43.782 --> 00:39:46.262
She stares at me all day.


00:39:46.303 --> 00:39:49.423
Like one of those vultures
on Animal Planet.


00:39:54.504 --> 00:39:56.985
She's scared.


00:40:05.184 --> 00:40:08.905
Yes, sweetie, well, I'm doing
the best that I can, okay?


00:40:08.946 --> 00:40:12.066
Then tell him, just tell him
I'm gonna be there for lunch.


00:40:12.106 --> 00:40:15.306
Yes, Lise, I'm leaving now.


00:40:15.345 --> 00:40:18.226
Okay, I'll see you then. Bye.


00:40:21.147 --> 00:40:23.747
- Yup.
- Mr. Jensen?


00:40:23.787 --> 00:40:27.868
I have Mr. Blackridge for you.


00:40:27.908 --> 00:40:30.668
Put him through.


00:40:30.707 --> 00:40:32.588
Hey! How's Prague?


00:40:32.627 --> 00:40:36.108
- Give me the number, fucknuts.
- Just north of one-fifty.


00:40:36.148 --> 00:40:39.188
Halfway through the month?
Christ, Jensen.


00:40:39.229 --> 00:40:43.188
Is Wilson at her desk or is she
moving her shit into my office?


00:40:43.229 --> 00:40:46.550
Kafka was right. Prague just
won't seem to let me go.


00:40:46.589 --> 00:40:50.189
- Tell me about Ryan.
- He's much improved.


00:40:50.230 --> 00:40:53.710
- Should I be worried?
- No. No, he's...


00:40:53.749 --> 00:40:56.831
Put Wilson on. I want to make
sure she mailed Bob a copy


00:40:56.870 --> 00:40:58.711
of his non-compete.


00:40:58.751 --> 00:41:05.311
Hey. I got Ed for you.


00:41:05.351 --> 00:41:07.512
I checked on your Dr. Singh.


00:41:07.552 --> 00:41:10.071
He's a heavy hitter, one of the best.


00:41:10.112 --> 00:41:11.472
Thanks, Lynn.


00:41:11.512 --> 00:41:13.112
I got you an early Christmas present.


00:41:13.152 --> 00:41:15.192
In your "throw-down" drawer.


00:41:23.273 --> 00:41:26.113
I'm about to shove November
straight up your ass.


00:41:29.513 --> 00:41:31.394
Wish I'd done that.


00:41:31.433 --> 00:41:34.193
Which one of you meat-eaters


00:41:34.234 --> 00:41:36.715
is getting an interview today!? Come on!


00:41:44.874 --> 00:41:46.876
Mr. Jensen.


00:41:48.075 --> 00:41:49.955
What's up, doc?


00:41:49.995 --> 00:41:52.555
I've heard that before.


00:41:52.595 --> 00:41:54.836
You're here late.


00:41:54.876 --> 00:41:58.596
I spoke with your wife earlier.


00:41:58.636 --> 00:42:01.277
Your son's initial response to chemo is


00:42:01.317 --> 00:42:03.676
less than encouraging.


00:42:03.716 --> 00:42:05.437
Meaning what?


00:42:05.476 --> 00:42:07.957
We should discuss options
still available to us...


00:42:07.998 --> 00:42:10.397
Still available? "Still"?


00:42:10.437 --> 00:42:13.638
Gene therapy. Using viruses


00:42:13.677 --> 00:42:16.478
to alter Ryan's T cells
and attack the cancer.


00:42:16.519 --> 00:42:19.399
I've had excellent success
in a handful of cases


00:42:19.438 --> 00:42:21.559
using measles, the common cold...


00:42:21.599 --> 00:42:25.078
You want to treat Ryan's cancer


00:42:25.118 --> 00:42:28.320
with, what... measles?


00:42:28.359 --> 00:42:31.360
HIV. An engineered form.


00:42:31.399 --> 00:42:36.001
We're not in some
third world country, no offense.


00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:40.401
This is Chicago.
We're in a state-of-the-art hospital!


00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:42.561
What do we need to do to


00:42:42.601 --> 00:42:45.882
in the realm of non-bat shit
treatments-to turn this around?


00:42:45.921 --> 00:42:49.602
Cancer is not a negotiation, Mr. Jensen.


00:42:51.362 --> 00:42:53.842
Goodnight.


00:43:27.006 --> 00:43:30.405
So, focus on his multiplication tables.


00:43:30.446 --> 00:43:33.086
- Try and make a game out of it.
- Okay.


00:43:33.125 --> 00:43:35.927
Ryan knows what to do
with these worksheets.


00:43:35.967 --> 00:43:39.966
Shh, shh, shh! Quiet study.


00:43:40.006 --> 00:43:42.046
Here we go.


00:43:42.087 --> 00:43:44.607
When you...


00:43:44.646 --> 00:43:47.527
If you need more, I can always


00:43:47.568 --> 00:43:49.447
send them home with Lauren.


00:43:52.327 --> 00:43:54.767
This is from all of us.


00:43:54.807 --> 00:43:57.408
Thank you. Okay.


00:43:57.449 --> 00:43:59.529
Okay, sweetheart.


00:44:04.248 --> 00:44:07.689
Okay. Let's talk about
the Continental Congress.


00:44:07.729 --> 00:44:10.530
- Who can tell me what that is?
- Excuse me.


00:44:10.569 --> 00:44:13.489
Whose paper towels are those?


00:44:13.530 --> 00:44:15.410
They belong to the class...


00:44:15.451 --> 00:44:19.131
Could we maybe put them someplace else?


00:44:19.171 --> 00:44:20.931
Mrs. Jensen, they're just...


00:44:20.970 --> 00:44:23.651
I know that the kids
need Kleenex, I just


00:44:23.691 --> 00:44:27.252
don't think they need
to be on Ryan's desk.


00:44:29.732 --> 00:44:31.931
Mrs. Jensen, no one's using the desk.


00:44:31.971 --> 00:44:34.170
Uh, sweetie, do you have room
in your own desk for these?


00:44:34.171 --> 00:44:37.373
- Hey, give me that.
- Uh...


00:44:38.452 --> 00:44:41.732
Take it easy, lady! Geez!


00:44:41.773 --> 00:44:44.212
Keep your shit off my kid's desk!


00:44:54.013 --> 00:44:58.454
Okay.


00:45:49.660 --> 00:45:50.939
Good to go, little man.


00:45:50.979 --> 00:45:54.099
What's going on?
Where's he going?


00:45:54.139 --> 00:45:57.060
209, South LaSalle.


00:46:10.141 --> 00:46:12.501
Come on,


00:46:12.541 --> 00:46:15.463
let's get you inside.


00:46:15.502 --> 00:46:17.982
"Completed in 1888,


00:46:18.022 --> 00:46:21.503
"the Rookery was designed
by architects Burnham and Root.


00:46:21.542 --> 00:46:25.143
It's the oldest high-rise
still standing in Chicago."


00:46:31.823 --> 00:46:34.864
Dane Jensen. Wyatt!


00:46:34.904 --> 00:46:37.304
Talk to me, babe.


00:46:37.344 --> 00:46:39.504
Sure they've got problems at the plant,


00:46:39.544 --> 00:46:41.343
is there a manufacturing company
in America that doesn't?


00:46:41.344 --> 00:46:42.384
Dad?


00:46:42.424 --> 00:46:45.506
Wyatt? Yeah, hold on a sec.


00:46:45.545 --> 00:46:48.666
- Thanks. What's up, buddy?
- Daniel Burnham?


00:46:48.705 --> 00:46:52.226
One of the guys who designed
this place? He said this,


00:46:52.265 --> 00:46:55.466
"Make no little plans,


00:46:55.507 --> 00:46:58.586
they have no magic to stir men's blood."


00:46:58.626 --> 00:47:01.147
Cool.


00:47:01.186 --> 00:47:04.106
Stir men's blood!


00:47:05.948 --> 00:47:09.467
Wyatt? Yeah, listen...
Yeah, I was having lunch


00:47:09.508 --> 00:47:13.667
one day in Talladega with
Richard Petty when... yeah,


00:47:13.707 --> 00:47:17.668
that Richard Petty.
Anyway, King Richard says to me...


00:47:17.708 --> 00:47:19.988
"Make no little plans, Dane,


00:47:20.028 --> 00:47:23.349
for they have no magic
to stir the blood."


00:47:23.388 --> 00:47:25.069
Well, I'll tell you what it means.


00:47:25.109 --> 00:47:27.190
It means if you want to continue


00:47:27.229 --> 00:47:31.109
to work a safe comfortable job,
then by all means stay at Rycon.


00:47:31.149 --> 00:47:33.069
But if you want a challenging position,


00:47:33.110 --> 00:47:35.151
one that will not only
define your career,


00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:38.390
but define you as a leader
of men, then this...


00:47:38.430 --> 00:47:41.550
This... is your chance!


00:47:41.591 --> 00:47:44.632
Well, of course if the money's right.


00:47:44.671 --> 00:47:47.471
Forget who you're talking to?


00:47:52.631 --> 00:47:54.752
Wyatt?


00:47:54.792 --> 00:47:57.632
I'll call you back.


00:49:04.720 --> 00:49:08.879
Mmm! Mom, I think our boy's
doing 100% better today.


00:49:08.919 --> 00:49:11.319
Some days are good, some days
are bad, but today...


00:49:11.359 --> 00:49:13.039
Is a good day.


00:49:13.079 --> 00:49:15.561
That's right. So, did they
bring your lunch up for you yet?


00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:17.440
Uh, no.
My dad's coming.


00:49:17.481 --> 00:49:18.800
Okay.


00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:20.079
I asked him if we could go somewhere.


00:49:20.080 --> 00:49:23.320
- Nice.
- Did you want to come, Mom?


00:49:23.360 --> 00:49:26.081
No. No, Nathan needs to see his mother.


00:49:26.122 --> 00:49:28.361
Besides, it's you and your dad's thing.


00:49:31.121 --> 00:49:33.442
Are you gonna be okay if I get going?


00:49:33.482 --> 00:49:34.802
Your dad will be here any minute.


00:49:34.842 --> 00:49:37.643
Sure.


00:49:37.682 --> 00:49:41.322
Do you know how much I love you?


00:49:43.404 --> 00:49:45.883
My first baby.


00:49:45.923 --> 00:49:48.123
You made me a mother.


00:49:48.163 --> 00:49:50.443
You taught me how to be a mom
to Lauren and Nate


00:49:50.483 --> 00:49:53.083
- I'll be okay, Mom.


00:49:53.123 --> 00:49:55.404
What?


00:49:55.444 --> 00:49:57.484
Of course you will!


00:49:57.524 --> 00:50:01.964
Silly.


00:50:05.365 --> 00:50:08.405
Have fun.


00:50:49.009 --> 00:50:52.169
You closing a deal without me?


00:50:52.209 --> 00:50:54.770
Huh? Michigan Avenue ain't coming to us.


00:51:03.091 --> 00:51:04.971
"The Tribune Tower,


00:51:05.011 --> 00:51:07.251
"designed in 1922


00:51:07.291 --> 00:51:09.491
by Howells and Hood."


00:51:11.331 --> 00:51:14.412
Huh. Listen, Rhino,
"More than 100 rock fragments


00:51:14.452 --> 00:51:17.012
from famous sites around
the world are embedded


00:51:17.052 --> 00:51:18.532
into the walls of the Tower."


00:51:18.572 --> 00:51:20.492
Look! The Alamo.


00:51:20.532 --> 00:51:23.333
Look, Edinburgh castle...


00:51:23.373 --> 00:51:27.093
Taj Mahal...
Great Wall of China? Are you kidding me?


00:51:30.013 --> 00:51:32.974
Is that one from
that building in New York?


00:51:33.014 --> 00:51:37.054
Sure enough.


00:51:37.094 --> 00:51:40.054
Where were we when that happened?


00:51:40.094 --> 00:51:43.095
We?


00:51:43.135 --> 00:51:46.215
You weren't even born yet.


00:51:48.375 --> 00:51:51.015
Someone heard a plane
hit the Trade Center.


00:51:51.055 --> 00:51:53.736
We thought it was an accident.


00:51:53.776 --> 00:51:56.616
Until the second one.


00:51:58.456 --> 00:52:00.936
We all stood and watched
on a TV in the conference room


00:52:00.976 --> 00:52:03.217
as the towers fell.


00:52:03.257 --> 00:52:05.137
Were you sad?


00:52:05.177 --> 00:52:08.497
For the people in the buildings, yeah.


00:52:08.537 --> 00:52:10.097
For little boys like you...


00:52:10.137 --> 00:52:12.537
who wouldn't see their
moms and dads again.


00:52:47.581 --> 00:52:51.541
Do you have to go back to work now?


00:52:51.581 --> 00:52:55.782
Yeah... I do.


00:53:06.463 --> 00:53:10.463
Okay, last but not least.


00:53:10.503 --> 00:53:13.384
So nice. Oh, wait, wait. Hold on.


00:53:13.424 --> 00:53:16.384
I thought it would be nice
if we went around the table


00:53:16.423 --> 00:53:18.144
and everyone could say something


00:53:18.184 --> 00:53:19.824
that they're thankful for.


00:53:19.864 --> 00:53:22.385
- Oh, that's a great idea.
- You know, it's Dane's house.


00:53:22.425 --> 00:53:24.865
- Why don't you go first?
- Yeah.


00:53:26.465 --> 00:53:29.625
Well, I'm, uh,


00:53:29.665 --> 00:53:33.705
thankful for...


00:53:36.546 --> 00:53:39.106
Excuse me.


00:53:41.826 --> 00:53:45.467
What a cornholing.
Wow, is she reaming your ass.


00:53:45.506 --> 00:53:48.747
How much more you gonna
put on for the month?


00:53:48.786 --> 00:53:51.627
I've probably got another fifteen.


00:53:51.666 --> 00:53:53.388
You don't want the job? That it?


00:53:53.428 --> 00:53:55.507
Yes, yes, I want the job, Ed.


00:53:55.548 --> 00:53:58.348
- You like working a desk then.
- No, I don't.


00:53:58.387 --> 00:54:01.028
You've got roughly four weeks.
Fond of you or not,


00:54:01.068 --> 00:54:03.389
I'm voting with my wallet, we clear?


00:54:03.429 --> 00:54:05.189
Yeah.


00:54:08.669 --> 00:54:12.630
- How's our boy?
- He's good.


00:54:12.669 --> 00:54:15.510
- Thanksgiving?
- Uneventful.


00:54:15.549 --> 00:54:19.310
Why didn't you ever marry, Ed?


00:54:19.350 --> 00:54:22.751
Have kids, do the whole family thing?


00:54:22.791 --> 00:54:26.311
I know me. I know I'm
the most interesting person


00:54:26.351 --> 00:54:27.871
you've ever met.


00:54:27.911 --> 00:54:30.431
And the most selfish.
I only want what I want.


00:54:30.471 --> 00:54:34.232
- Ever regret it?
- You mean like


00:54:34.272 --> 00:54:36.752
when it's Thanksgiving
and I'm here at the office...


00:54:36.791 --> 00:54:38.392
talking with fucknuts...


00:54:38.432 --> 00:54:41.232
instead of being surrounded
by people who give a shit?


00:54:41.272 --> 00:54:44.873
Yeah, maybe... But then again,


00:54:44.913 --> 00:54:47.632
you're not flying to Manhattan


00:54:47.673 --> 00:54:50.913
with a $5,000-a-night Brazilian
who's gonna turn your suite


00:54:50.953 --> 00:54:53.514
at the Carlyle into
a sexual Slip 'n Slide.


00:54:55.234 --> 00:54:57.754
See ya on Monday, kid.


00:55:07.835 --> 00:55:11.115
I had to take it... Ed.


00:55:11.155 --> 00:55:12.916
No, you didn't.
It's Thanksgiving.


00:55:12.956 --> 00:55:14.196
To Ed it's Thursday.


00:55:14.235 --> 00:55:17.196
To you it's Thursday!


00:55:17.236 --> 00:55:20.796
We're your family, Dane!
Those people in that room! Not Ed!


00:55:20.835 --> 00:55:23.197
This is my chance
to get off a desk, Elise.


00:55:23.237 --> 00:55:24.637
This is the job...


00:55:24.676 --> 00:55:27.556
I am so sick of your
"this is the job" excuse!


00:55:27.597 --> 00:55:29.957
Well, fine! You don't want me
to work so much? Great!


00:55:29.997 --> 00:55:32.278
After dinner, we'll put
your resume together...


00:55:32.318 --> 00:55:33.478
and you go get a job!


00:55:33.518 --> 00:55:35.797
Hmm? "Elise Katherine Jensen!


00:55:35.838 --> 00:55:39.077
"Ten years' experience
as a stay-at-home mom!


00:55:39.118 --> 00:55:42.759
Skilled in potty training,
play dates, and Pinterest!"


00:55:42.799 --> 00:55:45.798
Oh yeah, the job market's
just gonna snap you up!


00:55:45.839 --> 00:55:48.199
Hey, maybe you can go down
to St. Cecilia's,


00:55:48.239 --> 00:55:50.318
see if they'll buy that
"Doctor Mom" bullshit...


00:55:50.359 --> 00:55:51.640
and put you on the payroll.


00:55:51.679 --> 00:55:54.480
Then you can spring
for the cranberry sauce,


00:55:54.520 --> 00:55:57.840
or the white quartz
you just had to have,


00:55:57.879 --> 00:55:59.760
or the clothes you wear,


00:55:59.799 --> 00:56:02.400
or, or the ten grand "loan"


00:56:02.441 --> 00:56:04.040
we gave your brother two years ago...


00:56:04.081 --> 00:56:06.201
that he seems to have forgotten about.


00:56:06.240 --> 00:56:09.481
Hell, Elise, look how lucky we are,
we don't ever have


00:56:09.520 --> 00:56:10.961
to tell the kids there's no Santa...


00:56:11.001 --> 00:56:13.162
because as long
as I can magically continue


00:56:13.202 --> 00:56:15.402
to pull the mortgage out of my ass,


00:56:15.441 --> 00:56:17.601
I'm Santa fucking Claus 365 days a year!


00:56:29.763 --> 00:56:33.764
I lost the month, Elise.


00:56:33.803 --> 00:56:36.403
I took October,


00:56:36.444 --> 00:56:38.844
lost November.


00:56:38.883 --> 00:56:41.364
Driving around Chicago,
touring buildings


00:56:41.405 --> 00:56:43.845
instead of closing deals.


00:56:43.885 --> 00:56:46.525
Now I've got December to get Ed's job,


00:56:46.564 --> 00:56:51.686
and Christmas to New Year's
is a deal-killing nightmare.


00:56:56.126 --> 00:56:59.725
I'm sorry that you think
I've taken you for granted.


00:57:02.247 --> 00:57:05.727
I know you work hard for us.


00:57:05.766 --> 00:57:09.367
And you're probably right, Dane,


00:57:09.407 --> 00:57:13.008
the job that I could find
is not what you or Ed


00:57:13.047 --> 00:57:15.608
would consider worthwhile.


00:57:15.647 --> 00:57:19.367
My skills are not what anyone


00:57:19.408 --> 00:57:22.648
would consider "marketable."


00:57:26.409 --> 00:57:29.969
But I know how to prioritize.


00:57:51.092 --> 00:57:53.211
You okay over there?


00:57:53.252 --> 00:57:54.972
No.


00:58:28.535 --> 00:58:32.656
- Holy crap.
- Words right outta my mouth.


00:58:52.378 --> 00:58:56.938
- What do we know?
- The designer is Frank Gehry.


00:58:56.977 --> 00:58:59.818
The guy also did a museum in Spain,


00:58:59.857 --> 00:59:03.219
and he probably did
some other stuff too...


00:59:03.259 --> 00:59:07.059
But I do remember a quote of his.


00:59:07.099 --> 00:59:11.060
"When everybody else
is ready for the ending,


00:59:11.100 --> 00:59:13.299
I'm just ready to begin."


00:59:13.339 --> 00:59:17.260
I like Frank.


00:59:17.299 --> 00:59:21.140
Me too, buddy.


00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:23.740
Hey, Dad?


00:59:23.780 --> 00:59:26.340
Yeah?


00:59:26.380 --> 00:59:30.302
Do you believe in God?


00:59:34.142 --> 00:59:37.181
Depends on the month I'm having.


00:59:37.221 --> 00:59:39.941
Me too.


00:59:46.063 --> 00:59:49.183
Did you have a girlfriend
when you were as old as me?


00:59:51.023 --> 00:59:54.783
Why do you ask?


00:59:54.824 --> 00:59:59.143
Cause there's this girl
in my class. Her name


00:59:59.184 --> 01:00:01.344
is Shawna.


01:00:01.385 --> 01:00:05.225
I think she likes me.


01:00:07.344 --> 01:00:09.544
Shawna, huh?


01:00:09.584 --> 01:00:12.185
Sounds exotic.


01:00:16.425 --> 01:00:19.745
<i>He'll get worse before he gets better...</i>


01:00:19.786 --> 01:00:20.986
<i>Worse?! How can he...</i>


01:00:21.027 --> 01:00:22.787
<i>Ryan's cancer is unusually aggressive.</i>


01:00:22.827 --> 01:00:23.946
- Virus!
- HIV.


01:00:23.986 --> 01:00:26.546
<i>He's telling us our boy's going to die.</i>


01:00:26.586 --> 01:00:28.505
<i>Don't you get it? Jesus, Dane,
what are we going to do?</i>


01:00:28.506 --> 01:00:30.267
<i>Cancer is not a negotiation, Mr. Jensen.</i>


01:00:33.427 --> 01:00:36.108
No, twenty people lose
their job, it's a layoff.


01:00:36.147 --> 01:00:37.787
Just you, it's a termination.


01:00:37.827 --> 01:00:39.707
I told you that before the interview.


01:00:39.747 --> 01:00:41.228
The top end is 70.


01:00:41.268 --> 01:00:43.629
Forty years old,
a wife and three kids...


01:00:43.669 --> 01:00:45.189
how do you not pass a drug test?


01:00:45.228 --> 01:00:47.388
When I started recruiting
on this, you told me


01:00:47.428 --> 01:00:48.827
there were five must-haves for this job.


01:00:48.828 --> 01:00:52.109
Your guy has one.
Maybe. I can't justify the fee.


01:00:53.029 --> 01:00:55.349
It's about three now,


01:00:55.389 --> 01:00:57.510
so I expect he'll be back
from his meeting...


01:00:57.549 --> 01:00:59.710
- I don't know, maybe five?
- Would you ask Lou


01:00:59.749 --> 01:01:01.911
to call me when he's...


01:01:01.950 --> 01:01:04.590
Uh, what kind of meeting is it?


01:01:04.631 --> 01:01:07.911
He got a call from another
head... from another recruiter.


01:01:07.951 --> 01:01:10.711
- It was just last night.
- Bernadine?


01:01:10.751 --> 01:01:11.792
Hello?


01:01:11.831 --> 01:01:13.751
- Bernadine, hey.
- Dane.


01:01:13.791 --> 01:01:17.512
- Bernadine? Hello?
- Sumner tells me


01:01:17.552 --> 01:01:19.111
Lou has an interview?
That's wonderful!


01:01:19.151 --> 01:01:21.312
You're not upset?


01:01:21.353 --> 01:01:23.152
Upset? I'll be the first one
to congratulate him,


01:01:23.153 --> 01:01:24.592
no matter how he finds a job.


01:01:24.632 --> 01:01:27.352
You're joking, right?
Listen up, monkeys,


01:01:27.393 --> 01:01:30.834
I need fresh meat to peddle, stat!
Process Engineer.


01:01:30.873 --> 01:01:33.834
Oh, he is very excited, Dane.


01:01:33.874 --> 01:01:36.553
It's an Engineering Manager spot.


01:01:36.594 --> 01:01:40.474
That's fantastic! Scratch that!
Engineering Manager!


01:01:40.514 --> 01:01:43.274
The drive's a little long, but doable.


01:01:43.315 --> 01:01:46.154
Gimme a drive-in, 423 area code!


01:01:46.195 --> 01:01:49.434
But Dane, it means
we wouldn't have to sell.


01:01:49.475 --> 01:01:52.675
I mean, we're just... we're just hoping.


01:01:52.715 --> 01:01:56.435
So am I. You're both
in my family's prayers.


01:01:56.476 --> 01:01:59.155
Bernadine...


01:01:59.195 --> 01:02:02.276
What company is he interviewing with?


01:02:02.316 --> 01:02:04.276
Now, I'm not a pushy man
by nature, Paul,


01:02:04.317 --> 01:02:06.956
ordinarily I don't get this excited


01:02:06.996 --> 01:02:08.596
about a candidate.
But when I told my guy


01:02:08.636 --> 01:02:10.437
that I was going to call you, he went...


01:02:10.478 --> 01:02:13.837
pardon my vulgarity...ape shit.
- I don't know.


01:02:13.877 --> 01:02:17.157
Degreed engineer, really
mature for such a young man...


01:02:17.198 --> 01:02:19.998
no wife, no kids, easy relo.


01:02:20.038 --> 01:02:23.278
But the best thing is, he knows exactly


01:02:23.318 --> 01:02:25.038
what you guys are trying to do
over there...


01:02:25.039 --> 01:02:26.838
and he wants to be a part of it.


01:02:26.878 --> 01:02:29.719
Now what time tomorrow morning
do you want him to be there?


01:02:29.759 --> 01:02:31.519
I think we might have found
the seasoning...


01:02:31.559 --> 01:02:32.560
we've been looking for.


01:02:32.599 --> 01:02:34.480
Seasoning's good.


01:02:34.519 --> 01:02:38.119
But some clichés are clichés for
a reason. Particularly the one


01:02:38.159 --> 01:02:40.680
about old dogs, new tricks.


01:02:42.841 --> 01:02:45.360
Eight AM sharp, Mr. Cobra.


01:02:59.843 --> 01:03:02.723
Dr. Singh?


01:03:07.162 --> 01:03:11.203
- How are you, my friend?
- Tired.


01:03:11.244 --> 01:03:13.643
The T cells we treated


01:03:13.683 --> 01:03:16.483
are fighting the cancer in you.


01:03:18.323 --> 01:03:22.485
Dr. Singh? Your hat...


01:03:22.524 --> 01:03:25.404
Why do you wear it?


01:03:27.445 --> 01:03:30.605
I am a Sikh.


01:03:30.645 --> 01:03:34.045
My turban represents my constant
devotion to my religion.


01:03:36.086 --> 01:03:38.485
What do Sikhs believe?


01:03:41.086 --> 01:03:43.726
It's my experience


01:03:43.767 --> 01:03:47.366
that all religions are
some version of your, uh,


01:03:47.406 --> 01:03:49.767
'what goes around comes around.'


01:03:57.608 --> 01:04:00.889
We believe in the dignity
of everyone regardless


01:04:00.928 --> 01:04:05.048
of race, age, man, woman.


01:04:05.088 --> 01:04:07.889
A devout Sikh would protect


01:04:07.928 --> 01:04:10.770
with his own life the poor and the weak.


01:04:12.609 --> 01:04:15.729
Ryan...


01:04:15.769 --> 01:04:19.090
If I could take your sickness


01:04:19.131 --> 01:04:23.490
on myself and away from you,


01:04:23.531 --> 01:04:25.810
I would.


01:04:38.652 --> 01:04:40.533
Hey, buddy.


01:04:40.572 --> 01:04:42.892
Hey, Dad.


01:04:42.932 --> 01:04:45.492
Are you missing your party?


01:04:47.572 --> 01:04:50.493
Hey, honey, how you feeling?


01:04:50.533 --> 01:04:53.014
I'd like to go somewhere.


01:05:10.176 --> 01:05:14.095
There you go, Rhino,
the Wrigley Building.


01:05:14.136 --> 01:05:17.375
Man, that's a lotta Juicy Fruit.


01:05:17.415 --> 01:05:19.656
What do we know, Dad?


01:05:19.696 --> 01:05:22.976
Let's see what we got.


01:05:24.977 --> 01:05:27.776
It was modeled after
the Seville Cathedral's...


01:05:27.816 --> 01:05:29.817
Giralda Tower in Spain.


01:05:29.857 --> 01:05:32.977
Built in 1921.


01:05:33.017 --> 01:05:37.897
It has 250,000 terracotta tiles on it...


01:05:37.937 --> 01:05:41.178
that's why it's so white.


01:05:41.218 --> 01:05:46.218
That and the 116,000-watt
bulbs pointing at it.


01:05:46.258 --> 01:05:49.619
That clock? The hour hand


01:05:49.659 --> 01:05:53.459
is just over six feet long,
the minute hand nine feet.


01:05:53.499 --> 01:05:57.139
How do you know all that?


01:05:57.179 --> 01:06:00.980
There's a bank on the fourth floor.


01:06:01.020 --> 01:06:03.540
I was a teller there
for a little over a year...


01:06:03.580 --> 01:06:04.820
when I was seventeen.


01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:08.421
- You worked in there?
- Mm-hmm.


01:06:08.462 --> 01:06:11.941
Awesome.


01:06:11.981 --> 01:06:14.381
Did I know that?


01:06:18.222 --> 01:06:21.622
It was like going to work in a castle.


01:06:21.662 --> 01:06:25.302
Only seventeen.


01:06:25.342 --> 01:06:29.943
And in such a hurry to be older.


01:06:29.983 --> 01:06:32.903
Get on with my life.


01:06:38.664 --> 01:06:41.464
I'm glad we came.


01:06:56.705 --> 01:06:59.226
♪ I can see your eyes ♪


01:06:59.266 --> 01:07:03.666
♪ Underneath these bright city lights ♪


01:07:03.706 --> 01:07:09.626
♪ Everything's all right
for a change... ♪


01:07:10.241 --> 01:07:12.907
Last call at the bar, ladies
and gentlemen. Last call.


01:07:23.068 --> 01:07:26.828
- You clean up good.
- It's the dress.


01:07:26.868 --> 01:07:30.589
- Oh yeah, I see it now.
- Asshole.


01:07:31.869 --> 01:07:33.549
How's Ryan?


01:07:36.509 --> 01:07:39.830
- How long did Ed last?
- About an hour.


01:07:39.870 --> 01:07:41.790
Stretched it out, did he?


01:07:44.110 --> 01:07:47.270
You did an amazing job.
Situation reversed,


01:07:47.310 --> 01:07:50.551
sick child, I don't know...
- Doing an amazing job.


01:07:50.591 --> 01:07:54.231
Doing.


01:07:56.071 --> 01:07:58.632
You're solid, Wilson.


01:07:58.672 --> 01:08:03.032
You don't cut corners,
you do it the right way.


01:08:03.072 --> 01:08:05.712
Wow.


01:08:05.752 --> 01:08:09.313
You know, for all your macho BS,


01:08:09.353 --> 01:08:11.073
you're the first person
there every morning


01:08:11.113 --> 01:08:12.833
and the last one to go home.


01:08:12.873 --> 01:08:14.592
You're on the phone
from the minute you get in,


01:08:14.593 --> 01:08:16.713
to the minute you leave.


01:08:16.753 --> 01:08:17.953
You're the biggest producer
at the company


01:08:17.954 --> 01:08:19.354
because you're the hardest worker,


01:08:19.394 --> 01:08:21.554
not 'cause any of that
Dixie Mafia nonsense.


01:08:25.114 --> 01:08:28.515
We're not that different, you and I.


01:08:30.355 --> 01:08:32.955
- You're so full of shit.
- We're very different.


01:08:32.995 --> 01:08:35.835
- We're totally different.
- Yeah.


01:08:35.875 --> 01:08:38.795
Anyway,


01:08:38.836 --> 01:08:43.556
the month's not over.
I may have a few tricks left.


01:08:45.396 --> 01:08:47.917
No. You don't.


01:08:47.957 --> 01:08:51.757
You're down $20,000 with
ten live interviews on the board.


01:08:51.797 --> 01:08:53.556
Five are wet because
your people are scared of you.


01:08:53.557 --> 01:08:55.277
They're phantoms. I checked.


01:08:55.317 --> 01:08:58.678
That leaves five remaining shots.
Of those, one pays a fee


01:08:58.718 --> 01:09:00.597
that would blow me out of
the water but it's a first interview


01:09:00.598 --> 01:09:02.078
for a VP level position,


01:09:02.118 --> 01:09:04.117
which means it's rolling into next year.
Four shots left.


01:09:04.118 --> 01:09:06.558
Of those, three are interviewing
for the same job.


01:09:06.598 --> 01:09:09.079
Triple coverage, place one,
that's 15,000.


01:09:09.119 --> 01:09:10.438
Which leaves you
with one live interview,


01:09:10.439 --> 01:09:12.359
which, by the way, looks great.


01:09:12.398 --> 01:09:14.919
But the offer is contingent
upon a drug test.


01:09:14.959 --> 01:09:18.080
A drug test he can't pass.
How do I know?


01:09:18.120 --> 01:09:20.600
I heard him tell your guy
about his coke habit


01:09:20.640 --> 01:09:21.880
when I tapped into the call.


01:09:21.920 --> 01:09:25.280
You did what?


01:09:25.320 --> 01:09:28.481
You thought I was going
to rely on the honor system


01:09:28.521 --> 01:09:30.681
with a man who named himself
after a venomous snake


01:09:30.721 --> 01:09:33.201
and keeps a drawer
full of burner phones?


01:09:39.362 --> 01:09:42.722
Merry Christmas, Wilson.


01:09:42.762 --> 01:09:45.242
Go fuck yourself.


01:10:00.604 --> 01:10:04.324
Oh, Ryan, Daddy's here.


01:10:05.644 --> 01:10:08.605
Hey, Rhino.


01:10:08.644 --> 01:10:10.525
Hey...


01:10:10.564 --> 01:10:13.685
You're gonna be all right.


01:10:13.725 --> 01:10:16.365
I promise you.


01:10:16.405 --> 01:10:18.606
You hear me?


01:10:18.645 --> 01:10:23.045
I... I got the Frank Lloyd Wright books.


01:10:23.086 --> 01:10:25.806
And we're gonna go and see
the Thomas Gale House,


01:10:25.846 --> 01:10:28.847
you and me.


01:10:41.687 --> 01:10:43.688
Hi.


01:11:54.695 --> 01:11:57.056
Ryan Jensen's room number, please.


01:11:57.096 --> 01:11:58.136
Are you family, sir?


01:11:58.176 --> 01:12:00.896
Absolutely.


01:12:04.752 --> 01:12:06.857
The ventilator will breathe
for Ryan until such time


01:12:06.897 --> 01:12:08.777
as you decide to stop it.


01:12:08.817 --> 01:12:11.416
Or until he breathes for himself, right?


01:12:11.457 --> 01:12:13.617
Until such time as
he breathes for himself?


01:12:13.657 --> 01:12:16.498
We need to discuss your wishes


01:12:16.538 --> 01:12:18.778
when Ryan codes again.


01:12:18.817 --> 01:12:22.937
- When his heart stops.
- Correct.


01:12:22.978 --> 01:12:26.179
What's to discuss?
It stops, you start it.


01:12:26.218 --> 01:12:29.739
Ryan is in a coma. He's tired.


01:12:29.778 --> 01:12:32.819
Okay, we got it, thanks.


01:12:46.181 --> 01:12:49.421
Mr. and Mrs. Jensen.
Few things


01:12:49.461 --> 01:12:52.781
are as comforting
for a child as the sound


01:12:52.820 --> 01:12:56.821
of a mother and father's voice.
Perhaps...


01:12:56.862 --> 01:13:00.381
Perhaps you can give Ryan
the chance to heal,


01:13:00.422 --> 01:13:04.382
let the treatment fight the leukemia...


01:13:04.422 --> 01:13:07.102
Please.


01:13:15.302 --> 01:13:18.864
Are you kidding me?
"Sound of a voice"?


01:13:18.903 --> 01:13:21.184
- Stop it.
- Surrounded by millions


01:13:21.223 --> 01:13:23.904
of dollars' worth
of technology and he says


01:13:23.943 --> 01:13:26.625
"sound of a voice"!
- I said, stop it!


01:13:29.665 --> 01:13:33.105
He's right.


01:13:33.144 --> 01:13:36.106
Kids need their parents, Dane.


01:13:36.146 --> 01:13:38.345
Not just when they're sick.


01:13:41.306 --> 01:13:45.306
Oh, I love Ryan every bit
as much as you do, Elise.


01:13:45.345 --> 01:13:48.587
I know you do.


01:13:48.627 --> 01:13:50.507
I just wish it didn't take him dying


01:13:50.547 --> 01:13:51.787
in order to get your attention.


01:13:51.826 --> 01:13:55.547
That's not fair.


01:13:55.587 --> 01:13:59.188
No, it's not fair.


01:13:59.227 --> 01:14:02.748
But my little boy is dying,


01:14:02.787 --> 01:14:06.549
so I'm not terribly concerned
about what's fair.


01:14:09.429 --> 01:14:11.629
I love you, Dane, but I'll be damned


01:14:11.669 --> 01:14:13.269
if I'm gonna let you keep him hooked up


01:14:13.309 --> 01:14:16.349
to some machine in order
to make up for lost time.


01:15:01.274 --> 01:15:04.274
I'm sorry about Ryan.


01:15:06.954 --> 01:15:09.915
I was with him when
he got his lumbar puncture.


01:15:09.955 --> 01:15:13.115
You know, his spinal tap?


01:15:20.715 --> 01:15:24.156
Anyway, I'm studying for my RN,


01:15:24.196 --> 01:15:28.477
so I try to expose myself
to as many procedures as I can.


01:15:28.516 --> 01:15:32.476
Plus, it helps the kids, right?
When they see a familiar face.


01:15:32.516 --> 01:15:35.237
I keep their mind off
the needle with questions...


01:15:35.278 --> 01:15:38.718
What's Dad do at work,


01:15:38.758 --> 01:15:42.357
what grade they're in,
sport they like to play,


01:15:42.398 --> 01:15:44.717
favorite food,


01:15:44.758 --> 01:15:47.199
just, you know...


01:15:47.239 --> 01:15:49.119
Stuff.


01:15:49.159 --> 01:15:51.759
I'm gonna ask you
another question, Ryan.


01:15:51.798 --> 01:15:54.278
What's your favorite place
in the world, buddy?


01:15:54.318 --> 01:15:56.320
Hmm? Come on, you're doing fine, Ryan.


01:15:56.359 --> 01:15:58.080
What's your favorite place?


01:15:58.119 --> 01:16:00.479
- My house.
- Your house?


01:16:00.520 --> 01:16:03.759
<i>There's a place on our step
which lets me know</i>


01:16:03.800 --> 01:16:05.761
<i>when Dad's coming up.</i>


01:16:07.480 --> 01:16:10.161
<i>He's the only one big enough
to make the noise.</i>


01:16:10.200 --> 01:16:12.960
<i>He works late a lot.</i>


01:16:13.001 --> 01:16:17.562
<i>But if I leave my light on,
he'll always come in.</i>


01:16:17.601 --> 01:16:20.402
<i>I tell him it's because I like to draw,</i>


01:16:20.442 --> 01:16:22.882
<i>but I really just want to see him.</i>


01:16:22.922 --> 01:16:25.602
<i>When he leaves,</i>


01:16:25.643 --> 01:16:28.482
<i>I can still smell
his cologne for a while.</i>


01:16:28.523 --> 01:16:31.442
<i>It smells like Dad.</i>


01:16:31.482 --> 01:16:33.723
<i>It makes me feel...</i>


01:16:33.763 --> 01:16:36.044
<i>Safe.</i>


01:16:36.084 --> 01:16:39.764
Anyway, I just thought
you'd want to hear it.


01:16:41.684 --> 01:16:45.365
I think I would if my kid
someday thinks I'm a good dad.


01:16:45.405 --> 01:16:47.565
Like yours must.


01:16:52.844 --> 01:16:56.366
Antoine.


01:16:56.405 --> 01:17:00.206
My kid ever say what I did?


01:17:00.245 --> 01:17:03.045
For a living?


01:17:05.046 --> 01:17:07.686
Help other dads get jobs


01:17:07.727 --> 01:17:10.927
so they can take care of their families.


01:18:02.171 --> 01:18:05.052
The Thomas Gale House
is important, not only


01:18:05.093 --> 01:18:07.612
because it showcases the earlier
work of Frank Lloyd Wright,


01:18:07.652 --> 01:18:10.772
but it also hints at his
architectural potential.


01:18:10.812 --> 01:18:12.972
Now, Wright designed the home


01:18:13.012 --> 01:18:15.094
just before he left
the firm Adler and Sullivan.


01:18:15.133 --> 01:18:17.653
He didn't leave.


01:18:17.694 --> 01:18:21.053
Sullivan fired him.


01:18:21.093 --> 01:18:22.214
Because of this house.


01:18:22.254 --> 01:18:23.973
Excuse me, sir, can I help you?


01:18:24.014 --> 01:18:27.295
It's a "bootleg."


01:18:27.334 --> 01:18:31.254
Frank designed it
off the books for the Gale Family.


01:18:31.295 --> 01:18:34.375
See, before, Frank was all


01:18:34.416 --> 01:18:37.815
Prairie Style hard lines


01:18:37.856 --> 01:18:40.935
and cold concrete.


01:18:40.975 --> 01:18:43.935
He designed spaces
that let the light in.


01:18:43.975 --> 01:18:47.457
Rooms that opened up,
then wrapped around you.


01:18:47.496 --> 01:18:52.937
Made a family feel protected, safe.


01:18:52.977 --> 01:18:56.977
Like you belonged here
and only here. Home.


01:18:57.017 --> 01:19:00.337
But Frank was also a gambler,
a risk taker, and he knew


01:19:00.378 --> 01:19:02.256
that if he designed this
off the books for the Gale family,


01:19:02.257 --> 01:19:04.698
his boss might find out.


01:19:06.539 --> 01:19:09.498
Yeah, he knew what
goes around comes around.


01:19:11.339 --> 01:19:14.379
But he also knew that
he could create something


01:19:14.419 --> 01:19:17.179
that would live forever.


01:19:17.220 --> 01:19:19.700
Something magical.


01:19:23.059 --> 01:19:26.860
The magic that stirs men's blood.


01:19:38.141 --> 01:19:41.102
- Wheeler residence.
- Lou, Dane.


01:19:42.941 --> 01:19:46.102
About time you got off your ass
and went back to work, isn't it?


01:19:46.142 --> 01:19:48.223
Christmas is over, buddy.


01:19:48.262 --> 01:19:50.782
We got a week to get you a job
this year. Chop, chop.


01:19:50.823 --> 01:19:52.423
Think of him as a forty-year-old, Jimmy,


01:19:52.462 --> 01:19:54.944
we'll throw in the other
nineteen years free of charge!


01:19:54.983 --> 01:19:56.703
Hold that thought, Jimmy.


01:19:56.744 --> 01:20:00.263
Dane Jensen.
Hey, Toni, thanks for calling back,


01:20:00.304 --> 01:20:03.184
appreciate it.
Yes, Lou Wheeler.


01:20:03.223 --> 01:20:06.504
No, no, no! Don't you get it?
Every other company in the South


01:20:06.544 --> 01:20:09.064
is thinking first of the year!
You see him now,


01:20:09.104 --> 01:20:12.144
you take him off the market
before they get a crack at him!


01:20:12.184 --> 01:20:13.985
Now, what time Wednesday
works best for you?


01:20:14.026 --> 01:20:15.106
Morning or afternoon?


01:20:15.145 --> 01:20:18.585
Lou? Lou, calm down.


01:20:18.625 --> 01:20:21.426
Tell me, what are you saying
when they ask you,


01:20:21.465 --> 01:20:24.027
"Tell me a little bit about yourself?"


01:20:24.066 --> 01:20:26.146
No, Lou.


01:20:26.186 --> 01:20:29.706
Lou, no, Lou, Lou, stop!


01:20:29.746 --> 01:20:33.466
Christ, brother,
this isn't the biography channel.


01:20:33.508 --> 01:20:36.187
Even I wouldn't hire you.


01:20:36.227 --> 01:20:38.867
Alright.


01:20:38.907 --> 01:20:41.027
Okay. Say this.


01:20:42.867 --> 01:20:45.788
Miss Smith... Toni?


01:20:45.828 --> 01:20:50.229
You know, I've been working
a long time, you can see that.


01:20:50.268 --> 01:20:53.989
And all those years, I've seen
a lot of people come and go.


01:20:55.829 --> 01:20:59.789
I've seen people chasing what
they think will make them happy,


01:20:59.830 --> 01:21:03.470
money, titles, whatever.


01:21:03.510 --> 01:21:05.871
But it seems to me,


01:21:05.910 --> 01:21:08.071
if a man, or a woman,


01:21:08.110 --> 01:21:12.031
can be paid an honest wage
for an honest job,


01:21:12.070 --> 01:21:15.192
and if you can look back
at what you've done


01:21:15.231 --> 01:21:18.751
and feel proud,


01:21:18.791 --> 01:21:22.151
well then, you're happy.


01:21:23.992 --> 01:21:26.912
I know I'm running the risk of
sounding a little old fashioned,


01:21:26.952 --> 01:21:29.873
or naive even, but...


01:21:29.912 --> 01:21:33.553
I don't think you can
ever be too qualified


01:21:33.593 --> 01:21:36.833
to be happy. Do you?


01:21:36.874 --> 01:21:42.873
No, Lou, I don't.


01:21:42.914 --> 01:21:47.074
So. Tell me a little bit about yourself.


01:21:57.595 --> 01:22:01.075
- Dane Jensen.
- Dane, Toni. You know,


01:22:01.115 --> 01:22:02.874
I've never seen a tighter race
between two candidates.


01:22:02.875 --> 01:22:04.636
We'd like to make an offer.


01:22:07.236 --> 01:22:09.116
He'll take it.


01:22:09.156 --> 01:22:12.117
Whoa, pardner...
Shouldn't I tell you what it is first?


01:22:12.156 --> 01:22:15.558
Yeah, all right.


01:22:15.597 --> 01:22:18.557
Okay, what, what, what are you thinking?


01:22:18.597 --> 01:22:20.117
Well, we're kicking up the pay grade.


01:22:20.157 --> 01:22:22.477
One hundred grand base
salary, full relo,


01:22:22.517 --> 01:22:25.358
no sign-on bonus. Insurance
to start first day of employment.


01:22:25.398 --> 01:22:27.758
Now, it is New Year's Eve and
I plan to start drinking early.


01:22:27.759 --> 01:22:29.558
My math says your fee's 25,000.


01:22:29.598 --> 01:22:31.278
I need that expense
on this year's budget,


01:22:31.319 --> 01:22:34.279
so what do you say we wrap
this up right now. Is he in or out?


01:22:37.319 --> 01:22:40.839
On behalf of Mr. Wheeler, I accept.


01:22:40.879 --> 01:22:43.080
Mr. Wheeler?
No, no, Dane, look,


01:22:43.121 --> 01:22:45.640
we all loved Lou, but he came in second.


01:22:45.680 --> 01:22:46.879
I'm talking about your other guy.


01:22:46.880 --> 01:22:48.680
Other guy? What other guy?


01:22:48.720 --> 01:22:52.400
Ping. As in pong. You know,
the engineer with the doctorate.


01:22:52.440 --> 01:22:54.041
Ping?


01:22:54.081 --> 01:22:56.041
Ten minutes after I schedule
Lou for an interview,


01:22:56.081 --> 01:22:58.441
we get this resume from your
colleague at Blackridge.


01:22:58.482 --> 01:23:01.761
You don't forget a name
like "Race Bannon".


01:23:01.801 --> 01:23:04.802
Just, uh...


01:23:04.842 --> 01:23:08.562
Hang on one sec, Toni.


01:23:08.602 --> 01:23:10.482
Bannon?


01:23:10.522 --> 01:23:13.043
What?


01:23:16.323 --> 01:23:18.362
It doesn't really matter to me
which candidate you hire.


01:23:18.363 --> 01:23:20.083
Good 'cause we're hiring Ping.


01:23:20.123 --> 01:23:23.604
Bannon's my guy, so his deal's my deal.


01:23:23.644 --> 01:23:26.084
I win either way.


01:23:26.124 --> 01:23:28.164
I win.


01:23:28.204 --> 01:23:31.204
What else you got for Lou?


01:23:31.244 --> 01:23:33.245
Position wise? Nothing.


01:23:33.285 --> 01:23:36.685
Plain Jane Engineer, entry level,
temp to hire, anything?


01:23:36.725 --> 01:23:38.685
No, no, and no.


01:23:38.725 --> 01:23:42.165
Look, email me an invoice, Dane,
I gotta run. Happy New Year!


01:23:42.205 --> 01:23:43.887
Wait, who was the better candidate?


01:23:43.926 --> 01:23:45.326
What?


01:23:45.366 --> 01:23:47.766
Who was the best candidate?


01:23:49.606 --> 01:23:52.567
Are you recording this?


01:23:55.447 --> 01:23:59.007
Okay. Fact is,
Lou's the better candidate.


01:23:59.047 --> 01:24:01.407
Now, I can't ask him his age,
but come on...


01:24:01.447 --> 01:24:03.808
<i>How many years does he have
before he calls it a day?</i>


01:24:03.848 --> 01:24:05.408
<i>He's just...</i>


01:24:05.448 --> 01:24:09.208
He's not a good investment.
Between realtor fees,


01:24:09.248 --> 01:24:12.409
closing costs, storage,
relo's gonna run us $40,000,


01:24:12.449 --> 01:24:14.921
temporary living another ten,
and then there's your fee?


01:24:17.129 --> 01:24:19.929
Hire Lou,


01:24:19.969 --> 01:24:21.969
and he's on the house.


01:24:23.810 --> 01:24:25.810
I didn't hear you right.
Say that again?


01:24:25.850 --> 01:24:28.090
You heard right.


01:24:28.130 --> 01:24:31.970
Hire Bannon's guy you pay 25 grand.
Hire Lou? No charge.


01:24:32.010 --> 01:24:34.051
What?


01:24:34.091 --> 01:24:38.491
Why in the world would you
wanna give away 25,000.


01:24:38.531 --> 01:24:41.531
Because he's a good man.


01:24:41.571 --> 01:24:45.252
He has experience no degree can match.


01:24:45.292 --> 01:24:48.492
He's made all the mistakes a guy
half his age hasn't made yet,


01:24:48.532 --> 01:24:52.092
but will, and he's learned
from them all.


01:24:52.133 --> 01:24:56.213
Because he won't lie or pretend
to be something he's not.


01:25:01.213 --> 01:25:05.054
Because everything I need,
I already have.


01:25:05.094 --> 01:25:08.854
And because the price is right, Toni.


01:25:08.894 --> 01:25:13.335
It's a one-time offer,
expires in ten seconds.


01:25:13.375 --> 01:25:15.495
In or out?


01:25:18.335 --> 01:25:21.295
Who was that?


01:25:23.136 --> 01:25:25.136
Lou, what's wrong?


01:25:29.616 --> 01:25:32.057
What would you think
about seeing the grandkids?


01:25:33.897 --> 01:25:36.177
I thought we were gonna wait
until money wasn't so tight.


01:25:36.217 --> 01:25:38.857
I don't mean visiting them,
I mean seeing them.


01:25:38.897 --> 01:25:40.857
Every day, if you want to.


01:25:40.897 --> 01:25:43.938
That was Miss Smith from LOK.


01:25:43.978 --> 01:25:46.378
They're based out in Gainesville.


01:25:46.418 --> 01:25:50.698
That's about, what?
Half an hour away from the kids?


01:25:50.738 --> 01:25:53.339
I'm their new Engineering Manager.


01:25:55.299 --> 01:25:57.579
Gosh!


01:25:57.618 --> 01:26:00.579
Louis William Wheeler,


01:26:00.619 --> 01:26:04.900
I am so proud of you.
Oh gosh, honey.


01:26:04.940 --> 01:26:08.660
Come on now, come on...


01:26:08.700 --> 01:26:11.660
We both knew it was only
a matter of time.


01:26:13.981 --> 01:26:18.301
Well, I have so much packing
to do, and a realtor and...


01:26:18.341 --> 01:26:20.621
Can I call the kids and tell them?


01:26:20.660 --> 01:26:24.342
- Yeah! I'm gonna take a shower.
- Okay.


01:27:36.669 --> 01:27:39.309
Well, Rhino...


01:27:39.349 --> 01:27:42.190
We did it.


01:27:52.991 --> 01:27:56.031
I don't know what I thought.


01:27:56.071 --> 01:27:59.191
Maybe if I did a good thing,


01:27:59.231 --> 01:28:02.792
something totally out of character,


01:28:02.832 --> 01:28:06.392
that it would make a difference.


01:28:06.431 --> 01:28:11.433
And it did, I guess,


01:28:11.473 --> 01:28:13.952
to a man I've never seen.


01:28:13.993 --> 01:28:16.753
Probably never talk to again.


01:28:19.312 --> 01:28:21.274
But I did something today


01:28:21.314 --> 01:28:24.793
that you'd be proud of.


01:28:24.834 --> 01:28:27.994
I mean, I think you'd be proud of me.


01:28:30.954 --> 01:28:33.675
Just like I'm proud of you.


01:28:33.714 --> 01:28:37.875
Something I should have
told you every day...


01:28:37.915 --> 01:28:39.795
I'm proud


01:28:39.835 --> 01:28:42.915
of my little man.


01:28:44.756 --> 01:28:48.076
And if you're tired of fighting,


01:28:48.115 --> 01:28:51.797
and... and you need to rest,


01:28:51.836 --> 01:28:55.597
well, then I understand.


01:28:57.436 --> 01:28:59.997
I'll even keep Lauren out of your room.


01:29:03.118 --> 01:29:06.158
Me and your mom...


01:29:06.198 --> 01:29:08.837
we'll be okay...


01:29:08.878 --> 01:29:12.439
We'll be okay.


01:29:31.361 --> 01:29:33.640
Hey, hon.


01:29:33.681 --> 01:29:36.361
How's he doing?


01:29:38.200 --> 01:29:40.842
The same.


01:29:44.162 --> 01:29:46.561
You need to get some rest.


01:29:46.602 --> 01:29:49.561
Let me sit with him for a while.


01:29:49.602 --> 01:29:53.643
My mom's got the kids.
Go home, take a nap.


01:29:53.682 --> 01:29:56.323
Maybe a shower?


01:29:56.363 --> 01:29:59.243
Please?


01:30:02.004 --> 01:30:04.324
I can't.


01:30:04.363 --> 01:30:06.443
I don't wanna be gone if he...


01:30:06.484 --> 01:30:10.044
Dane, if he codes,


01:30:10.084 --> 01:30:13.444
and only if he codes, I'll call you.


01:30:15.445 --> 01:30:18.644
I'm not gonna let him go without you.


01:30:56.528 --> 01:30:59.928
Dane?


01:30:59.969 --> 01:31:01.810
I love you.


01:31:01.849 --> 01:31:05.250
What's not to love?


01:32:37.419 --> 01:32:39.419
You're fired.


01:32:41.260 --> 01:32:43.779
What?


01:32:43.820 --> 01:32:46.779
You can't just fire me.


01:32:46.819 --> 01:32:50.861
My place. I'll fire anybody I want.


01:32:50.900 --> 01:32:55.101
No, I don't mean...
I don't mean you "can't," Ed, I mean...


01:32:55.140 --> 01:32:57.180
Pack up your shit and beat it.


01:33:00.702 --> 01:33:03.941
Fired? Why?


01:33:03.982 --> 01:33:08.461
Because I gave away one guy?
Ed, I've billed out millions...


01:33:08.502 --> 01:33:10.423
Wilson won.
She doesn't need you


01:33:10.463 --> 01:33:11.862
second-guessing her.


01:33:11.902 --> 01:33:13.501
And besides, you're not
the same guy anymore.


01:33:13.502 --> 01:33:16.862
- I... I am the same guy, Ed...
- No, you are not!


01:33:16.903 --> 01:33:20.143
I don't want to hear it anymore.


01:33:22.143 --> 01:33:25.784
Over. Done. Get out.


01:33:46.745 --> 01:33:49.106
You know, I used to say
a relationship with you


01:33:49.146 --> 01:33:52.506
was based only on the ability
to make you money.


01:33:54.347 --> 01:33:56.626
It just sounded tough.


01:33:56.667 --> 01:33:59.387
I never really believed it.


01:33:59.428 --> 01:34:03.027
Shut the door, please.


01:34:34.831 --> 01:34:39.391
No, no, no, no, no, no...


01:35:20.476 --> 01:35:22.116
Oh, honey!


01:35:22.155 --> 01:35:26.676
Dad?


01:35:35.676 --> 01:35:38.718
Rhi... Rhino!


01:35:38.757 --> 01:35:42.158
- How you doing?
- Fine.


01:35:49.279 --> 01:35:52.039
- Hey.
- It's gonna be okay.


01:35:53.878 --> 01:35:55.879
It's gonna be okay.


01:36:06.879 --> 01:36:09.680
Come here.


01:36:27.842 --> 01:36:30.762
How's he doing?


01:36:30.802 --> 01:36:33.962
His counts are still coming up.


01:36:34.003 --> 01:36:37.002
He's good.


01:36:37.042 --> 01:36:40.963
Uh, a messenger brought this
to the house. From Blackridge.


01:36:50.444 --> 01:36:53.124
Here's the thing, Elise.


01:36:53.164 --> 01:36:56.204
I didn't take today,


01:36:56.244 --> 01:37:00.445
or yesterday, or the week off.


01:37:00.486 --> 01:37:03.245
I lost my job.


01:37:03.285 --> 01:37:05.245
Ed fired me.


01:37:05.286 --> 01:37:08.166
Oh.


01:37:09.566 --> 01:37:12.166
Thank God!


01:37:34.648 --> 01:37:38.489
What is this?


01:37:44.090 --> 01:37:46.570
Son of a bitch.


01:38:20.213 --> 01:38:24.053
- Daddy!
- Jesus, Lauren...!


01:38:24.093 --> 01:38:25.573
Watcha doin'?


01:38:25.613 --> 01:38:28.174
I'm working.
What does it look like I'm doing?


01:38:28.214 --> 01:38:30.175
Doesn't look like you're doing anything.


01:38:30.214 --> 01:38:31.975
I'm making it look easy.


01:38:34.254 --> 01:38:36.734
- What?
- Can we sit in your spinny?


01:38:36.775 --> 01:38:39.175
No, you can't sit in my spinny chair.


01:38:39.215 --> 01:38:41.935
Please?


01:38:41.976 --> 01:38:44.815
Okay, come on.


01:38:48.136 --> 01:38:50.656
- Getting in?
- I'm too old for that.


01:38:50.696 --> 01:38:54.296
Come on!


01:38:54.336 --> 01:38:58.177
- Okay! Scooch over!
- Alright, here we go!


01:38:58.217 --> 01:39:02.377
No!


01:39:02.418 --> 01:39:04.537
Kids, come on, leave your dad alone.


01:39:04.577 --> 01:39:06.136
You gotta get on the bus
in half an hour.


01:39:06.137 --> 01:39:09.419
Come on. Lauren, that hair...


01:39:09.458 --> 01:39:12.538
Do you want the door open or shut?


01:39:12.578 --> 01:39:14.619
Will someone wipe me when I'm done?


01:39:14.658 --> 01:39:15.658
Shut.


01:39:41.981 --> 01:39:44.662
Jensen Group Recruiting, Dane Jensen.


01:39:44.701 --> 01:39:46.140
It's about time you got off your ass...


01:39:46.141 --> 01:39:47.742
and went back to work, isn't it?


01:39:47.782 --> 01:39:49.743
Lou, how you doin'?!


01:39:49.782 --> 01:39:52.343
Oh man, I'm busier than a one-legged man


01:39:52.382 --> 01:39:54.462
in an ass-kicking contest...
and I'm loving it!


01:39:56.062 --> 01:39:59.263
And how about you?
Can't be easy starting over again.


01:39:59.303 --> 01:40:02.503
Oh, I'm... I'm pretty busy.


01:40:02.543 --> 01:40:04.943
- Could you be busier?
- Come again?


01:40:04.983 --> 01:40:06.542
Now that I've got
my feet on the ground...


01:40:06.543 --> 01:40:08.824
I've got the go-ahead
to hire some new engineers.


01:40:08.864 --> 01:40:12.504
Engineers?


01:40:12.544 --> 01:40:14.424
Plural?


01:40:14.464 --> 01:40:16.944
Yeah, we're gonna need two
at least to start, maybe three.


01:40:16.984 --> 01:40:19.585
We're flexible on salary.


01:40:19.625 --> 01:40:21.024
And I wouldn't want to talk
to anybody but you, Dane.


01:40:21.025 --> 01:40:22.985
And I never will.


01:40:23.025 --> 01:40:26.585
I appreciate that, Lou. I do.


01:40:26.625 --> 01:40:30.146
And I'm thinking of one name,


01:40:30.186 --> 01:40:33.866
three words, Mohammed Al Farooq.


01:40:33.907 --> 01:40:37.547
- Oh, boy.
- Hey, Lou...


01:40:37.587 --> 01:40:40.067
You mind if I call you back
in about an hour?


01:40:40.107 --> 01:40:42.667
I've got a daily meeting I can't miss.


01:40:42.707 --> 01:40:44.027
Walnuts?


01:40:44.067 --> 01:40:45.747
Oz says they're his food of choice.


01:40:45.787 --> 01:40:47.987
If you're ever stranded on
a deserted island... walnuts.


01:40:47.988 --> 01:40:51.588
Guess he didn't plan
on being stranded with you.


01:40:57.509 --> 01:41:00.029
You save the receipt for this?


01:41:00.069 --> 01:41:01.429
I think it's here somewhere, why?


01:41:01.469 --> 01:41:02.749
Alright, yeah.


01:41:02.789 --> 01:41:05.629
Tax write-off... business lunch.


01:41:08.630 --> 01:41:11.870
Dane Jensen... husband, father,


01:41:11.910 --> 01:41:13.799
last of the romantics.

